A||||12||Sevaan||Spark Plugs||||8||
Z||000000||Sevaan||02-28-2001||07:30 PM||||Hey again guys. I want to replace the sparks in my engine, but they are stuck fast! Either they are ancient, or the previous own over-torqued them...now I've tried soaking it in WD-40, but it still won't budge. And this morning I took it to a garage so they could try and they said that if it break they will have to service the whole engine.<P>So I took the car back and it's soaking some more as we speak. Do you have any advice on the best way to remove them without them breaking?||||reg||8
Z||000001||Greg Piet||02-28-2001||08:05 PM||||What is gonna break?  the ceramic?  The threads and the hex part that the socket goes on should be 1 piece of metal...<P>I would try something besides wd40, like a magic rust remover, and also with that stuff get the motor warm, but make sure nothing ignites!!!<BR>then tap the head lightly with a hammer to get try to get the penetrating fluid to get in the threads...||||reg||1
Z||000002||Formula88||02-28-2001||09:32 PM||||Try a product called "Liquid Wrench" available at most auto parts stores.  I soaked my rusted exhaust in it over night and got the exhaust off without any trouble, or broken bolts!||||reg||1
Z||000003||Sevaan||02-28-2001||10:54 PM||||I've tried the Liquid Wrench stuff as well...They're in too tight!<p>[This message has been edited by Sevaan (edited 02-28-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Sevaan||02-28-2001||11:10 PM||||Oops, didn't see what you wrote, Greg. I'll give it a go.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Turbo1dr||02-28-2001||11:42 PM||||Try PB Blaster.<P>------------------<BR><B>78 Malibu</B> 3.8L Turbo Buick<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/turbowonder/Malibu/Homex.html" TARGET=_blank>TURBO WONDER</A><BR>Best 8th: 1.548, 6.62@106.32  3450lb<BR>TE-70 3 bolt, Ported Stock Iron Heads and Intake<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/turbowonder/Malibu/Modifications_files/Intercooler.jpg" TARGET=_blank>2.5 stock intercoolers</A><BR>Vernon Swindell, Jr.||||reg||1
Z||000006||mrfiero||03-01-2001||01:07 AM||||PB Blaster is great stuff.......so is Kroil.  That stuff is industrial grade!.<P>Don't worry too much if the ceramic part breaks up while trying to remove them......like Greg said, the threads and the hex part are one in the same.  <P>The problem is the threads of the spark plug could be "welded" to the threads in the head.  I saw this phenomenon when I worked for the Fiero Factory.  The car was an '86 GT with 50 or 60K miles on the original plugs.  The plugs came out, but the threads stayed behind.  Needless to say, the heads had to be removed.<P>I certainly hope you don't experience this.  Keep soaking and they should come out.  Good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Racer 1||03-01-2001||02:08 AM||||Use a breaker bar!<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=159982&a=1168563&p=42189251&Sequence=0"> ||||reg||5
Z||000008||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||03-01-2001||04:35 PM||||When you do get those plugs out . . .<P>Use autolite, champion, Nippon Denso, or whatever, but do not use Bosch Platinum.<P>My duke wanted to puke on the Plus-4s.  My buddies Cherokee ran kooky on the regular platinums.<P>I have tried them on a volvo v6 and they made it sick.  In my colt GT, bad performance and missfire did I see . . .<P>No, I am not Dr. Suess, but I am totally fed up with splitfires, platinum, ring fire, and every other gimicky plug out there.  Maybe the Delco rapid fires are good, but I am tired of trying out radical plugs. It's just not worth the money or time.<P>If a spark plug costs more than $2 each, then it is probably going to make your car run like crap.  <P>My duke loves autolite anything.  Starters, alternators, plugs . . . you name it.  If it says autolite on it then it will work great.<P>As for removing the plugs, I am sorry to hear that you are struggling.  I hope that you find a way to get them out of there!<P>Good luck and may the force be with you.  ||||reg||1
Z||000009||DaRkLoRD||03-01-2001||07:07 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by LuckyTheyWereEverMade:<BR><B>When you do get those plugs out . . .<P>Use autolite, champion, Nippon Denso, or whatever, but do not use Bosch Platinum.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Uh oh, here we go again.. LMAO!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||12
Z||000010||Indiana_resto_guy||03-01-2001||10:23 PM||||When you do get them out put anti-sieze or silicon lube on them. It will help next time around.<BR>Will it still run? Might try it warm.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Sevaan||03-02-2001||10:49 PM||||They're out! Thanks for the help guys.<P>Now my Fiero is a mean driving machine.||||reg||1
